re>
<p>We primarily need three components to send an iOS push notification:</p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>Provider. A provider builds and sends notification requests to Apple Pus=
h Notification Service (APNS). To construct a push notification, the provid=
er provides the following data:</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>Device token: This is a unique identifier used for sending push notifica=
tions.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>Payload: This is a JSON dictionary that contains a notification=E2=80=99=
s payload. Here is an example:</p>
</li>
</ul>
<pre><code>{
   "aps":{
      "alert":{
         "title":"Game Request",
         "body":"Bob wants to play chess",
         "action-loc-key":"PLAY"
      },
      "badge":5
   }
}
</code></pre>
<ul>
<li>
<p>APNS: This is a remote service provided by Apple to propagate push notif=
ications to iOS devices.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>iOS Device: It is the end client, which receives push notifications.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<h4 id=3D"android-push-notification">Android push notification</h4>
<p>Android adopts a similar notification flow. Instead of using APNs, Fireb=
ase Cloud Messaging (FCM) is commonly used to send push notifications to an=
droid devices.</p>
